(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-abnormal-cell-growth-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Abnormal Cell Growth and Differentiation Quiz 2 — Clin Path I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why does the deck say genes promoting self destruction of cells with damaged deoxyribonucleic acid are necessary?","choices":["To slow the rate at which stem cells commit to differentiation","To stop the damage becoming permanent in dividing cells","To limit the total number of cells an organ can contain","To supply nutrients to neighbouring cells as they proliferate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — apoptosis prevents a mutation being carried on into the daughter cells. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which genes are named as promoting repair of damaged deoxyribonucleic acid?","choices":["p16 and p53","BRCA-1 and BRCA-2","NF-1 and NF-2","Retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a minority of breast cancer patients carry an inherited mutation in one of them. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What do Nx, N0 and N1 to N2 or higher each mean?","choices":["Cannot be assessed; none; increasing number and range involved","Nx, no regional nodal metastasis; N0, nodes cannot be assessed; N1 upwards, distant metastasis","Nx, nodes not yet sampled; N0, one node involved; N1 upwards, two or more nodes","Nx, nodes removed surgically; N0, nodes present but normal in size; N1 upwards, enlarged nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — x always means cannot be assessed, 0 always means none found, and the numbers rise with the number and range of nodes involved. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What defines a neoplasm?","choices":["A change of one differentiated cell type into another under chronic irritation","An abnormal mass of tissue growing autonomously, self-perpetuating without physiologic growth stimuli","An abnormal mass of tissue that enlarges only while a physiologic growth stimulus is present","An enlargement of an organ produced by enlargement of its individual cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and the term is interchangeable with tumor. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which tumours are associated with an inherited alteration of the retinoblastoma protein?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis types 1 and 2, with tumours of the central and peripheral nervous system","Malignant melanoma","Familial adenomatous polyposis and colon cancer","Retinoblastoma, a rare childhood tumour of the eye, and osteosarcoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same tumour suppressor, two very different tumours. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which five features does the deck list for a benign tumour?","choices":["Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, abnormal differentiation, and invasion","Circumscribed, compresses, often encapsulated, well differentiated, no metastasis, slow","Ragged border, infiltrates surrounding tissue, no capsule, variably differentiated, may metastasize, grows rapidly","Autonomous growth, clonal origin, parenchyma and stroma, a single genetic alteration, no stimulus needed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — each has a malignant counterpart on the same slide: ragged border, infiltrates and invades, various degrees of differentiation, may metastasize, grows rapidly. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which inherited tumour suppressor alteration is associated with malignant melanoma?","choices":["Adenomatous polyposis coli","BRCA-1","p16, also called INK4a","Retinoblastoma protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — one of five hereditary examples the deck lists. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is a carcinoma showing squamous cell differentiation called?","choices":["A sarcoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","An adenocarcinoma","A papilloma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the other named pattern is glandular, which gives adenocarcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is hypoplasia, and what does it produce?","choices":["Change of one mature cell type into another mature cell type","Partial development of an organ, resulting in a functional deficiency","Complete absence of an organ from failure of primordial tissue formation","Shrinkage of a tissue or organ that formed and matured normally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — some organ forms, but not enough of it to work properly. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How is a body cavity defined for the purposes of seeding?","choices":["By the lymphatic drainage basin that serves the organs within it","By the venous return that leaves the organs within it","By the membranes covering the organs and the cavity wall","By the bony boundaries that enclose the space"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pericardium, pleura and peritoneum cover heart, lungs and abdominal organs respectively, and also line the cavity wall. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does the degree of differentiation of a neoplasm measure?","choices":["How rapidly the tumour mass is enlarging over time","How many of its cells are in the proliferative pool at any moment","How closely it resembles comparable normal cells in appearance and function","How far it has spread from the primary site within the patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — this is the basis of histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What distinguishes atrophy from hypoplasia?","choices":["Atrophy is enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge","Atrophy is disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape","Atrophy is shrinkage of a tissue or organ that had already formed and matured normally","Atrophy is partial development of an organ that never reached mature size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the timing is what separates them: hypoplasia is a developmental failure. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Where does the gene damage in carcinogenesis come from?","choices":["Inherited, and/or acquired from chemicals, radiation or microbes","It is always inherited through the germline and never acquired during life","It is always acquired during life and never inherited","It arises only from errors during deoxyribonucleic acid replication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the named sources are chemical carcinogens, ultraviolet and ionizing radiation, and microbial organisms: viruses and one bacterium. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is agenesis?","choices":["Complete absence of an organ, because the primordial tissue never formed","Complete absence of an organ, although the primordial tissue formed normally","Partial development of an organ, resulting in a functional deficiency","Shrinkage of an organ that had already formed and matured normally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the failure is at the very first step, before there is anything to develop. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which four features define dysplasia?","choices":["Orderly growth, typically mesenchymal; uniform cell size; preserved architecture; nuclei of normal size and staining","Complete replacement of one mature epithelium by another mature epithelium, with normal architecture retained","Invasion through the basement membrane with spread to regional lymph nodes","Disordered epithelial growth, varied cell size and shape, lost architecture, darker larger nuclei"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and it may progress to cancer, which is why dysplasia is called precancerous. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"On the deck's metaplasia figure, which epithelium changes into which?","choices":["Bladder transitional epithelium becoming squamous epithelium","Bladder squamous epithelium becoming transitional epithelium","Oesophageal squamous epithelium becoming columnar epithelium","Cervical columnar epithelium becoming glandular epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — squamous metaplasia, driven by chronic irritation. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A benign tumour arises from cartilage. Which category does it fall into?","choices":["Epithelial, since cartilage lines a surface","Glandular, so it would be called an adenoma","Neither, since the classification covers only epithelial tumours","Mesenchymal, since cartilage is supportive tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — connective tissue, adipose, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone are all listed. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"On what three things is staging based?","choices":["Tissue of origin, whether mesenchymal or epithelial, and the naming rule that follows","Primary lesion size, regional nodal spread, and blood-borne metastases","Degree of differentiation, mitotic rate, and nuclear pleomorphism","Patient age, performance status, and duration of symptoms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is exactly what T, N and M stand for. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What do initiators do in chemical carcinogenesis?","choices":["They cause sustained or enhanced proliferation of already-damaged cells","They repair damage already caused by another carcinogen","They trigger apoptosis in cells with damaged genomes","They cause permanent damage to deoxyribonucleic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — permanence is what distinguishes initiation from promotion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What kind of organism is Helicobacter pylori, and what does it cause in the stomach?","choices":["A Gram-positive bacterium that colonizes the stomach, causing acute ulceration and perforation","A virus that integrates its genome into gastric epithelial cells","A fungus that colonizes the gastric mucosa in immunosuppressed patients","A Gram-negative bacterium causing chronic gastritis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it colonizes the stomach and may lead to atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia. It is the one bacterium among the microbial causes in this deck. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does eradicating Helicobacter pylori do to cancer risk?","choices":["It increases the risk, because the inflammatory response is protective","It reverses established gastric adenocarcinoma","It reduces gastric cancer risk, and some early MALT lymphomas regress","It has no effect on cancer risk once colonization has occurred"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — evidence that the chronic inflammation itself is the carcinogenic driver. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"On the deck's abnormal tissue growth figure, what is the sequence from normal to cancer?","choices":["Normal, metaplasia, atrophy, carcinoma in situ, cancer","Normal, hypertrophy, hyperplasia, dysplasia, cancer","Normal, aplasia, hypoplasia, dysplasia, cancer","Normal, hyperplasia, mild dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, cancer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ is labelled as severe dysplasia on that figure. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which cancers is Epstein Barr virus associated with, and which cells does it infect?","choices":["Cervical carcinoma; it infects cervical epithelium and integrates its genome","Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma; it colonizes the stomach","B cell lymphomas and nasopharyngeal carcinoma; it infects B lymphocytes","Hepatocellular carcinoma; it infects hepatocytes and causes chronic injury with regeneration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it immortalizes them, which is the key step, and it also infects epithelial cells of the oropharynx. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which three hurdles does the deck name for a tumour cell that has penetrated the vasculature?","choices":["Penetration of the vasculature, survival in the circulation, and survival in a new organ","Penetration of the vasculature, evasion of apoptosis, and induction of angiogenesis","Invasion of the basement membrane, entry into lymphatics, and nodal colonisation","Initiation, promotion, and progression"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reaching the bloodstream is only the first of the three. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Through which vessels does haematogenous tumour spread typically occur?","choices":["Arteries, especially the aorta and its major branches","Lymphatic vessels at the tumour margin","Capillaries within the tumour stroma only","Veins, especially the portal vein and the inferior vena cava"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why cancers often spread to liver and lungs respectively. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is a carcinoma with a glandular growth pattern called?","choices":["A sarcoma","An adenocarcinoma","An adenoma","A squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the prefix names the pattern, the suffix names the malignancy and the epithelial origin. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How does the deck qualify the cancer stem cell idea?","choices":["As a conceptual framework rather than an absolute explanation","As a fully established mechanism proven for every solid tumour","As a hypothesis that has since been disproved","As applicable only to haematological malignancies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the slide is explicit that this is a model, not a settled account. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which three concepts does the hepatitis B slide emphasise?","choices":["Chronic inflammation, regenerative hyperplasia, genomic instability","Initiation, promotion, and progression","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, and abnormal differentiation","Self-renewal, immortality, and differentiation capacity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same triad also explains hepatitis C and Helicobacter pylori. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does Tis mean in the TNM system?","choices":["The primary tumour cannot be assessed","There is no evidence of a primary tumour","The lesion has not invaded through the basement membrane","The lesion is too small to be measured accurately"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — 'is' refers to in situ, and it is the same threshold the dysplasia diagram turns on. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What happens when a protooncogene mutates?","choices":["It becomes an oncogene, which encodes oncoproteins that promote continued uncontrolled growth","It becomes a tumour suppressor gene, which then inhibits cell growth excessively","It becomes a repair gene, which then introduces errors into the genome","It becomes silent, so the cell loses its ability to grow at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same machinery, with the brake removed. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
